# Ash-first admission

Start with ordinary Ash resources and add only what admission needs. Nothing here requires the
engine until the last step; every earlier cell is decided in Elixir.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_graphlaw, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## An ordinary resource

`Demo.Note` knows nothing about GraphLaw. It is a plain Ash resource on the ETS data layer.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Note do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets

  ets do
    private? true
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :body, :string, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :write do
      accept [:body]
    end
  end
end
```

## Add the extension

Adding admission is additive: list `AshGraphLaw.Resource` under `extensions`, declare an
admission, and point an action at it with `AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit`. The `:rdfs` step needs no
law module; the built-in step runs over the default projection of the changeset.

`api_token` is `sensitive?: true`. The default projection omits sensitive attributes and
arguments entirely.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Memo do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGraphLaw.Resource]

  ets do
    private? true
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :subject, :string, public?: true
    attribute :api_token, :string, public?: true, sensitive?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :file do
      accept [:subject, :api_token]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :memo_closure}
    end
  end

  graphlaw do
    admission :memo_closure do
      step(:rdfs)
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource Demo.Note
    resource Demo.Memo
  end
end
```

The plain resource still works, unchanged:

```elixir
note = Ash.create!(Demo.Note, %{body: "plain Ash, no admission"}, action: :write)
{note.body, Ash.read!(Demo.Note) |> length()}
```

## The projection is deterministic and offline

What the engine will observe is the default N-Triples projection of the changeset. It is a pure
function of the input: build the changeset, project it, hash it. No engine is involved.

```elixir
{:ok, admission} = AshGraphLaw.Admissions.fetch(Demo.Memo, :memo_closure)

changeset =
  Ash.Changeset.for_create(Demo.Memo, :file, %{subject: "Q3 plan", api_token: "s3cr3t-token"})

{:ok, projection} = AshGraphLaw.Projection.Default.data(changeset, admission: admission)

%{
  dialect: projection.dialect,
  sha256: :crypto.hash(:sha256, projection.text) |> Base.encode16(case: :lower),
  mentions_subject?: String.contains?(projection.text, "Q3 plan"),
  leaks_sensitive?: String.contains?(projection.text, "s3cr3t-token")
}
```

`leaks_sensitive?` is `false` by construction: redaction is omission, not masking.

Projecting the same input twice yields byte-identical text, which is what makes evidence
replayable:

```elixir
{:ok, again} = AshGraphLaw.Projection.Default.data(changeset, admission: admission)
again.text == projection.text
```

## Unknown admissions are refused, not ignored

Asking for an admission the resource never declared is a typed refusal with the known names
attached:

```elixir
{:error, refusal} = AshGraphLaw.Admissions.fetch(Demo.Memo, :no_such_admission)
{refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.details.known}
```

## Cross the engine boundary

Start the pool and file a memo. With the engine vendored (`mix ash_graphlaw.vendor`) the outcome
is evidence. Without it the outcome is a typed refusal and nothing is persisted. Either way it
is a value you can match on.

```elixir
case AshGraphLaw.Pool.start_link(size: 1) do
  {:ok, _pool} -> :started
  {:error, {:already_started, _pool}} -> :already_running
end

outcome = Ash.create(Demo.Memo, %{subject: "Q3 plan", api_token: "s3cr3t-token"}, action: :file)

case outcome do
  {:ok, memo} -> {:admitted, memo.subject}
  {:error, error} -> {:refused, AshGraphLaw.Error.codes(error)}
end
```

## Ontology-first

When the admission model is larger than a handful of resources, do not hand-maintain it. Model
the admissions in RDF, generate the resources and law modules with ggen, and treat the Elixir
as a projection. See `documentation/how_to/regenerate_with_ggen.md`.
